# Database migrations (Flower ERP)

When changing `apps/api/prisma/schema.prisma`:

1. Create or update the migration SQL (never skip reviewing `migration.sql`).
2. Inspect **defaults** on every enum/text column affected by `ALTER COLUMN TYPE`.
3. Inspect **destructive** operations: `DROP TABLE`, `DROP COLUMN`, enum value removal.
4. Add **data guards** (`DO $$ … RAISE EXCEPTION`) before removing enum values or dropping data.
5. Wrap destructive migrations in `BEGIN;` … `COMMIT;`.
6. Run `pnpm migration:safety`.
7. Run `bash scripts/test-migrations.sh` (add `migration.test.json` for risky DROP/enum migrations).
8. Do not mark the task complete without passing both checks.

## Enum value removal

**Never trust auto-generated enum-removal SQL.**

Always use this order per column:

1. `ALTER COLUMN … DROP DEFAULT` (skip only with `-- @no-default` and confirmed no default in prior migrations/schema)
2. `ALTER COLUMN … TYPE … USING (col::text::NewEnum)`
3. `DROP TYPE` old enum
4. `RENAME TYPE`
5. `ALTER COLUMN … SET DEFAULT` when a default existed

Reference migrations:

- `20260729150000_remove_unused_enum_values` (SalesChannel)
- `20260730120000_remove_transfers_counts_cash` (InventoryBatchSourceType)

## Annotations (use deliberately)

- `-- @destructive-reviewed` — intentional DROP TABLE/COLUMN after runtime removal
- `-- @data-guarded` — migration includes row guards
- `-- @no-default` — column has no server default; DROP DEFAULT not required

## Forbidden

- `DROP … CASCADE`
- `prisma migrate reset` on shared/production databases
- `db push --force-reset`
- Editing already-applied production migrations (except the one explicitly being recovered)

See `docs/database-change-workflow.md` and `docs/migration-safety-audit.md`.
